      "be1496823220_html": "Residence Nil Rate Band",
      "be12711936915_html": "<b>£175,000</b>",
      "be2100674247_html": "APR &amp; BPR (from April 2026)&nbsp;",
      "be81632382121_html": "<b>£1 million cap</b>",
      "be416074918_html": "Capital Gains Tax Rates",
      "be195115683136_html": "<b>18% or 24% - £3,000 exemption</b>",
      "be103651667_html": "Stamp Duty",
      "be57065681951_html": "<b>0% up to £125k (£300k for first-time buyers)</b>",
      "be891423012_html": "Employers' NICs",
      "be146633716010_html": "<b>15%</b>",
      "be455105585_html": "Unspent Pension Funds (from April 2027)",
      "be64883995714_html": "<b>40% IHT</b>",
      "be1122286020_html": "Streamlining the Path Ahead",
      "be731623972_html": "Effortless Probate Solutions",
      "be782734546_html": "Estate planning offers a strategic means to bypass probate, the legal process of administering a deceased person's will. By employing tools such as living trusts, payable-on-death accounts, and beneficiary designations, individuals can ensure that specific assets seamlessly transfer to intended beneficiaries without going through the probate court. This not only expedites the distribution of assets but also provides a more private and cost-effective approach, sparing loved ones from potential delays and complications associated with probate proceedings. Implementing a comprehensive estate plan tailored to one's circumstances is key to effectively bypassing probate and ensuring a smoother transfer of assets to heirs.",
      "be53352122858_html": "<i>\"By failing to prepare, you are preparing to fail.\"</i>",
      "be174208741126_html": "<i>- Benjamin Franklin</i>",
      "be203588820_html": "Average Process",
      "be87453585520_html": "<b>9 - 12 months</b>",
      "be1302591902_html": "Process if Wills Contested",
      "be133994510021_html": "<b>18 - 24 months</b>",
      "be1422617924_html": "Typical Legal fees",
      "be29047754418_html": "<b>1% - 5%</b><br>",
      "be475652921_html": "of the estate total value",
      "be1540140006_html": "Protecting Your Financial Future",
      "be628334771_html": "Navigating the Care Landscape",
      "be1004389606_html": "The risk of losing one's home to cover care home fees is a concern that many individuals face when navigating the complexities of long-term care. If an individual is unable to privately fund their care and does not qualify for public funding, they may need to self-fund the cost using their assets, including their home. In such cases, individuals may be required to sell their property to cover the expenses associated with residential care. This process is often a last resort, and local authorities may place a charge on the property, delaying the sale until after the individual's passing. Establishing a trust proactively cushions against escalating expenses.",
      "be1286916298112_html": "<div><i>\"Always plan ahead. It wasn't raining when Noah built the ark.\"</i></div><div><i>\n</i></div><div>\n</div>",
